## Offline Sync Architecture
- The app has a full offline sync engine in `sync_engine.py`. Do NOT duplicate its logic.
- Network status is managed via `is_online()` / `set_online()` in `sync_engine.py`. All repository methods in `data/repositories.py` route through this flag.
- Reads: When online, MySQL SPs execute + results are cached in `read_cache` (JSON blobs keyed by proc+args hash) AND full table replicas are maintained in SQLite. When offline, reads fall back to SQLite replica tables first, then the SP result cache.
- Writes: INSERTs on students/academic_periods/enrollments route to `log_offline_insert()` and return negative temp IDs. UPDATE/DELETE raise `OfflineModeError`.
- Inbound sync: `pull_mysql_to_sqlite()` downloads all essential MySQL tables into SQLite replicas. It runs automatically (a) after successful online login, and (b) on offline-to-online reconnection.
- Outbound sync: `sync_offline_queue_to_mysql()` pushes queued offline INSERTs to MySQL with FK cascading (temp ID resolution). Triggered automatically on reconnection.
- `main.py` polls network status every 8 seconds via `_poll_network()`. The header bar shows a live online/offline indicator.
- `init_db()` in `main.py` is wrapped in try/except so the app boots in offline mode when MySQL is unavailable.

## Repository Conventions
- All new read methods MUST include offline routing: check `is_online()`, fall back to `sqlite_read_all()` / `sqlite_read_one()` for raw SQL, or `get_cached_read()` for SP-based reads.
- All new write methods that use raw SQL (not via `_call_write`) MUST include `if not is_online(): raise OfflineModeError()` guards.
- INSERT methods that should work offline MUST route to `log_offline_insert()` and return the temp negative ID.
- When adding a new MySQL table that needs offline access, you MUST: (1) add a matching CREATE TABLE in `init_local_db()`, (2) add it to `_REPLICA_TABLES` in `sync_engine.py`, (3) add it to `REPLICA_TABLES` in `certificate_manager_api/main.py` (the `/replica/{table}` endpoint refuses unlisted tables).
- The client NEVER connects to MySQL. `BaseRepository._call_*` helpers go through the FastAPI gateway (`POST /sp/read`, `POST /sp/write`). A new stored procedure called through them MUST be added to `SP_READ_WHITELIST` or `SP_WRITE_WHITELIST` in `certificate_manager_api/main.py`, then deployed.
- Run `python tests/check_no_direct_mysql.py` after any change; it fails if client code imports `db`, `mysql.*` or `DBConfig`, or calls `requests.get/post/...` directly.
- Every client HTTP call MUST use `api_client.get/post/put/patch/delete` (shared keep-alive session, HTTPS verification with `certs/college_ca.crt`, `X-App-Key` header). Never call `requests.*` directly.
- User permissions are resolved ONCE at login into `app_session.access` (`nicegui_ui/state.py`: role_id, allowed_screens, permissions, dept_scope). Screens read `app_session.get_access()`; after changing roles/permissions call `app_session.refresh_access(...)`.
- Secrets never go in git: `certificate_manager_api/app_keys.json`, `certificate_manager_api/certs/`, `server_config.json` (holds the client app key). Templates: `app_keys.example.json`, `server_config.example.json`.
- `C:\AppServ\www` is Apache's web root: the deployed API folder MUST keep its `.htaccess` (`Require all denied`) so Apache never serves code, credentials or keys.
